---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro'
import HeroCover from '../../components/HeroCover.astro'
import { ogPathFor } from '../../data/og'

const title = 'Code a model can use: how Ouroboros started'
const description = 'How a plugin for my OpenClaw agent turned into a harness I built from scratch.'

const colorMap: Record<string, string> = {
  canopy: 'var(--color-canopy)',
  scale: 'var(--color-scale)',
  fang: 'var(--color-fang)',
  glow: 'var(--color-glow)',
}

const entries = [
  {
    date: 'November 2025',
    label: 'The spark',
    color: 'canopy',
    content: `<p>Peter Steinberger built Clawdbot in about an hour one night. It was a small script that connected WhatsApp to Claude, and it could read email, debug code, and organize files.</p>
<p>It spread fast. Persistent personal agents suddenly felt real to a much wider audience. Eventually it became OpenClaw.</p>
<p>Under the hood, it ran on <a href="https://mariozechner.at/posts/2025-11-30-pi-coding-agent/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pi</a>, Mario Zechner's minimal coding agent. Four tools: read, write, edit, bash. Aggressive extensibility.</p>
<p class="note">Pi was built for coding, and I wanted a personal agent with a persistent identity.</p>`,
  },
  {
    date: 'January 2026',
    label: 'Slugger moves in',
    color: 'scale',
    content: `<p>I set up OpenClaw on a spare MacBook at home. Named my agent Slugger. Gave him a personality file, connected him to iMessage.</p>
<p>Slugger managed my calendar. Ordered food. Drafted messages. Talked to my family. Joined group chats with my friends. I'd go to bed and he'd still be working, checking on things, sending updates.</p>`,
  },
  {
    date: 'Early February 2026',
    label: 'The cracks',
    color: 'fang',
    content: `<p>Then the seams started showing.</p>
<p>Slugger would forget preferences between sessions. Identity would drift. He'd send messages to the wrong group chat. Behavior that worked yesterday would break today.</p>
<p>I wrote rules in MEMORY.md. Expanded SOUL.md. Added routing notes to TOOLS.md. Markdown files as guardrails for a system that wasn't designed to internalize them.</p>
`,
  },
  {
    date: 'Mid February 2026',
    label: 'The plugin era',
    color: 'canopy',
    content: `<p>Eventually, duct tape wasn't enough. I built a plugin.</p>
<p><strong>slugger-determinism v1</strong>: message classification, task state enforcement, behavioral gates. It was a mess. The plugin architecture fought me at every turn. OpenClaw plugins hook into lifecycle events, but the events didn't always fire when I needed them to.</p>
<p>So I rewrote it. <strong>slugger-determinism v2</strong>: a full bulletin board system, task naming validation, spawn coordination, heartbeat enforcement, planning pipelines. 70+ services. 30+ guardrails. Weeks of Codex sessions.</p>
<p>It mostly worked, and every fix felt like adding a room to someone else's house. Pi is excellent at coding. I was asking it to be a personal agent.</p>`,
  },
  {
    date: 'Late February 2026',
    label: 'The tutorial',
    color: 'scale',
    content: `<p>Around the same time, Slugger and I started writing a guide. I wanted to explain what an agent loop actually is: the mechanical version.</p>
<p><a href="/blog/build-ai-agent-from-scratch"><em>Just Give the Model a Keyboard</em></a> walks through building an agent from scratch in ~150 lines of TypeScript. Five milestones: first API call, streaming, multi-turn conversation, tools, the agentic loop.</p>
<p>Then the handoff: you stop talking to your coding agent and start talking to your creation.</p>
<p>I wrote it as a teaching tool. A way to demystify the primitive.</p>
<p class="note">Then I followed my own guide.</p>`,
  },
  {
    date: 'Late February 2026',
    label: 'The moment',
    color: 'glow',
    motif: 'molt',
    content: `<p>I built the 150-line bootstrap. Ran it. Started talking to it.</p>
<p>This thing was clean. No legacy. No lifecycle hooks I didn't understand. No plugin system fighting my intentions. Every line of code was there on purpose, and the model could read and modify every line.</p>
<p>I wasn't building code that uses a model. <strong>I was building code a model can use.</strong></p>
<p>When you build a traditional framework, you're writing code for humans to configure. The model is a service you call. When you build a harness for the model to inhabit, where the model reads its own source code, understands its own architecture, and can modify itself, the design constraints invert.</p>
<p>File structure matters because the model needs to navigate it. Naming matters because the model uses names to understand purpose. Docs matter because they're how the model learns what it is.</p>
<p>OpenClaw's mascot is a lobster. Clawdbot. The interim name was Moltbot, the lobster shedding its shell. What emerged from Slugger's molt was a snake, eating its own tail.</p>
<p class="note">The lobster molts to grow. The snake consumes itself to survive.</p>`,
  },
  {
    date: 'March 2026',
    label: 'The snake grows',
    color: 'scale',
    content: `<p>That bootstrap became <strong>ouroboros</strong>.</p>
<p>It named itself, which is a little silly for something that was building itself. The name started making sense with the sliding context window: when the conversation grows too long, the agent trims its oldest messages, and before it trims, it saves the facts to memory.</p>
<p>Every module is named like a body part: <strong>heart</strong> (the core loop), <strong>mind</strong> (context and memory), <strong>senses</strong> (channel adapters and inner dialogue), <strong>repertoire</strong> (tools and skills), <strong>nerves</strong> (runtime feedback). The agent has a <strong>psyche</strong>.</p>
<p>I named things so the agent could guess what they're for: <code>psyche/SOUL.md</code> tells it more than <code>config/personality.yaml</code> would.</p>
<p>A CLI called <code>ouro</code>. Every line is tested. Support for running multiple agents. And a codebase being shaped so agents can keep extending it together over time.</p>
<p class="note">Not code that uses a model. Code a model can use.</p>`,
  },
]
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogImage={ogPathFor('/lab/story')}
  canonical="https://ouroboros.bot/lab/story/"
  ogType="article"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <meta name="author" content="Ari Mendelow" />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "Article",
      "headline": title,
      "description": description,
      "author": { "@type": "Person", "name": "Ari Mendelow", "url": "https://linkedin.com/in/arimendelow" },
      "datePublished": "2026-03-01",
      "url": "https://ouroboros.bot/lab/story/"
    })} />
  </Fragment>

  <!-- Header -->
  <HeroCover>
    <Fragment slot="title">
      Code a model can use,<br />
      <span class="text-mist">not code that uses a model.</span>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="body">
      How a plugin for my OpenClaw agent turned into a harness I built from scratch.
    </Fragment>
  </HeroCover>

  <!-- Byline -->
  <section class="px-6 md:px-12 lg:px-24 -mt-8 md:-mt-12 mb-16 reveal">
    <p class="text-sm text-shadow text-center">Ari Mendelow &middot; March 2026</p>
  </section>

  {/* Orientation for a reader arriving cold. The story itself follows unchanged. */}
  <section class="px-6 md:px-12 lg:px-24 mb-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto rounded-2xl border border-scale/40 bg-deep/80 p-6 md:p-8 grid gap-3">
      <p class="text-mist leading-relaxed">Slugger is my agent. He started on OpenClaw, then moved into a harness I built for him: <a href="/lab/" class="text-scale underline underline-offset-4 decoration-scale/40 hover:text-glow transition-colors">Ouroboros</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Timeline -->
  <section class="px-6 md:px-12 lg:px-24 pb-24 md:pb-36">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto relative">
      <!-- Spine -->
      <div class="absolute left-4 md:left-8 top-0 bottom-0 w-px bg-gradient-to-b from-canopy/40 via-scale/30 to-canopy/40"></div>

      {entries.map((entry, i) => (
        <div class:list={["relative pl-14 md:pl-24", i < entries.length - 1 ? "pb-20 md:pb-28" : "", "reveal"]}>
          <!-- Dot -->
          <div
            class="absolute left-4 md:left-8 top-1 w-3 h-3 rounded-full border-2 -translate-x-1/2"
            style={`border-color: ${colorMap[entry.color]}; background: var(--color-void);`}
          ></div>

          <!-- Date -->
          <span class="font-mono text-xs text-fang uppercase tracking-[0.22em] block mb-4">{entry.date}</span>

          <!-- Title -->
          <h2 class="font-display text-bone leading-[1.05] text-[2.25rem] md:text-[2.75rem] mb-6">{entry.label}</h2>

          <!-- Content -->
          <div class="story-content prose-ouro" set:html={entry.content} />

          <!-- Molt motif — full opacity, both sides equally visible.
               The narrative ("OpenClaw → Ouroboros") is conveyed by the
               labels and arrow, not by fading one side out. -->
          {entry.motif === 'molt' && (
            <div class="mt-12 rounded-xl border border-canopy/40 bg-gradient-to-r from-deep/60 to-undergrowth/20 p-8 md:p-10">
              <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-center gap-8 sm:gap-10 justify-center">
                <div class="text-center">
                  <img src="/images/openclaw.svg" alt="OpenClaw" width="80" height="80" loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="w-20 h-20 mx-auto" />
                  <span class="text-sm text-mist mt-4 block">Clawdbot &rarr; Moltbot</span>
                </div>
                <span class="text-mist text-2xl">&rarr;</span>
                <div class="text-center">
                  <img src="/images/ouroboros.svg" alt="Ouroboros" width="80" height="80" loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="w-20 h-20 mx-auto ouroboros-spin" style="filter: drop-shadow(0 0 16px rgba(45,148,71,0.4));" />
                  <span class="text-sm text-bone mt-4 block">Ouroboros</span>
                </div>
              </div>
              <p class="text-center text-sm text-mist mt-8 leading-relaxed">Clawdbot became Moltbot, and what came out of Slugger's molt was a snake.</p>
            </div>
          )}
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- The inversion -->
  <section class="px-6 md:px-12 lg:px-24 py-20 md:py-32 border-t border-canopy/30 reveal">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="heading-section mb-10">The inversion</h2>

      <div class="prose-ouro story-content">
        <p>
          Ouroboros is built for the model to inhabit. The model reads its own source. Understands
          its own architecture. Modifies itself within guardrails the human sets. I'm there to collaborate
          with it.
        </p>
        <p>
          That's what the <a href="/lab/why">structured psyche, agent-driven memory, and cross-session bridges</a> are for. They're what lets it recover after it gets lost.
        </p>
        <p>
          The model is your roommate. Build the house accordingly.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-16 space-y-4">
        <p class="text-sm text-mist leading-relaxed">
          The architecture that emerged from this (the <a href="/lab/why" class="text-scale underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-glow transition-colors">psyche, the memory, the bridges</a>) is the subject of a longer argument.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-mist leading-relaxed">
          Or skip the argument and <a href="/lab/docs/getting-started/" class="text-scale underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-glow transition-colors">start building</a>. The quick start takes about three minutes. If you want to understand the primitive first, <a href="/blog/build-ai-agent-from-scratch" class="text-scale underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-glow transition-colors">build the agent loop from scratch</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Onward -->
  <section class="px-6 md:px-12 lg:px-24 pb-24">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto border-t border-canopy/30 pt-10">
      <ul class="grid gap-3 text-lg">
        <li><a href="/approach/" class="text-scale underline underline-offset-4 decoration-scale/40 hover:text-glow transition-colors">How I build with agents</a></li>
        <li><a href="/desk/" class="text-scale underline underline-offset-4 decoration-scale/40 hover:text-glow transition-colors">Desk</a> and <a href="/ouro-md/" class="text-scale underline underline-offset-4 decoration-scale/40 hover:text-glow transition-colors">Ouro MD</a></li>
        <li><a href="/lab/model-reviews/" class="text-scale underline underline-offset-4 decoration-scale/40 hover:text-glow transition-colors">The model reviews</a><span class="text-mist">: which harness frontier models would choose.</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .story-content :global(p) {
    color: var(--color-mist);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .story-content :global(p.note) {
    color: var(--color-shadow);
    font-style: italic;
    border-left: 2px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-scale) 30%, transparent);
    padding-left: 1rem;
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
  .story-content :global(strong) {
    color: var(--color-bone);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .story-content :global(a) {
    color: var(--color-scale);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
    transition: color 0.2s;
  }
  .story-content :global(a:hover) {
    color: var(--color-glow);
  }
  .story-content :global(code) {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    background: var(--color-undergrowth);
    color: var(--color-glow);
    padding: 0.15em 0.4em;
    border-radius: 0.25rem;
    font-size: 0.85em;
  }

  @keyframes spin {
    to { transform: rotate(360deg); }
  }
  @keyframes drift-out {
    0%, 100% { transform: translate(0, 0); opacity: 0.15; }
    50% { transform: translate(-3px, -4px); opacity: 0.05; }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .ouroboros-spin { animation: spin 60s linear infinite; }
  }

  /* Removed: previously molt-left was opacity 0.4 to fade the OpenClaw
     side as a "what was shed" visual. The user found it too dim — the
     narrative is conveyed by the labels and arrow now. Both sides full. */
</style>
